#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124329 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/id #uuid "f7b85641-289b-57b2-9ffc-ff4c5e10025a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8572" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/dfc9ee546f72ad68cf116b8f67d165ba9b6ff6e77dce88f145dda93f13091441.png)\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124330 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "854f39a0-99b3-59f3-a9d7-4909850254ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-8573" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/7126688b8500e60258d29ab4b1b323ac956ad41b530c5723bcc71d60b11e73a0.png)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/f3460b47901f79529216c1440403e2115ff9047cb997070893b241cd938728de.png) we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/95d1bcb284138711bcf5afddb3f09c702e664fbdc7b256b0eaf81044f87403cf.png)\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/898d72a1a0cc123d1ff7ecb4c8d02f6951739a9498e36ff14ee44cb322952fb6.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "cb76e9dc-1195-58d3-8e58-a3421770265a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8574" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/8921e351c90855b6bf113a057eaa7e3ab97fbba8f22254463d894e7c4f89a4ed.png) \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{8}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/ff2073ee3866cfd3b40084ed4e676cc57554d9cd02b9d6e26cb7808f127fc56c.png)\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/55c840d71fa907a9b372801123fdc7f19be810f2f489a2b167b0860c84d1c2cc.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/3d52ed0fc48e2e20d268b6a49b099f8e3165f25707fa36aaf93c0e8b0a2c3b16.png)\nSetting  $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/ebb90bd7e57a148040b69a9fd6fd7d4f1b253e63734617e4e57b5177d7f9cda9.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/4e04b534ec76c446059ca5119822b7aa299e0ea24c0e842de64a976954a733f8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "7959b6aa-7ef0-5ba1-b74b-5e59481d0145" :question/math-academy-id "e-8575" :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/513e5adf220f2bce1441beb318dd9a731f83a292f45d51f0f8c333dbb89a9b93.png) You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A,$ and\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/6478455f2e5fcd99530dea4dae13f5df9a50208ccd53166a99249d4d5b722530.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/a4c0d2bb4a988598d60a45f605261c8b4d5bb30922251d821990f3464c2d56b4.png) are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$ and $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3},$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/2dd66c70838ad6604661616c9bdbe3ce2e6293131084e4330b8e65dd39749f45.png)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/f811ea4fc7419e4afd395f1832dddc98e6b4829c6a24a34d5fd51c402767a51b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f9669b-d6e8-59d0-b7d8-36a68c242396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360841} {:answer/id #uuid "7f41faac-8b4b-530d-9afc-6566d7e69e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360842} {:answer/id #uuid "54f0bceb-219b-544a-87d2-e80679163391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360843} {:answer/id #uuid "f6029480-361b-56ac-a6a5-fcbb5f8908c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360844} {:answer/id #uuid "17850b78-a0e8-5e3e-9890-d988acd7c78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f41faac-8b4b-530d-9afc-6566d7e69e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360842} :answer-field/id #uuid "35318c80-5f09-5750-bebd-8244d2b18e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1fa7f70-96f7-5add-9ed6-bf21512ccb8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140039" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ c & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ c & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=-2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58cceb92-58fe-5156-989f-0b2a057fc8bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306214} {:answer/id #uuid "62b3a655-cb21-5490-b639-285b81db3c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306215} {:answer/id #uuid "a848b508-0c63-53ba-aa21-33ee30a038f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306216} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9dd13c-21b2-53d9-bd93-6975ce578bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306217} {:answer/id #uuid "8834a7c9-ee24-5c0d-9840-34fb22108046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee9dd13c-21b2-53d9-bd93-6975ce578bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306217} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1b537c-5521-5da0-8038-c727415e4ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d92b551-0b27-59ea-861b-c8ea209a5b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140041" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-(1)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ea0d80-d8ed-5742-b580-acd17e924317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360848} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0d929e-1535-5edd-adea-6402afa67fbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360849} {:answer/id #uuid "46e1bdb9-47a0-58f8-83a8-1ef8d065d844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360850} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed6a480-f8b8-55c5-8817-b1751fbb6d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360851} {:answer/id #uuid "8c75cfe3-9149-5a88-becc-c45ae44dee53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d0d929e-1535-5edd-adea-6402afa67fbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360849} :answer-field/id #uuid "49cc2694-4ab7-58b9-8c75-08236df282a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c21ab48-f38e-5c23-b250-b441e717ec06" :question/math-academy-id "q-140053" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & 5 \\\\ b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c01e01-7ee8-52e8-8d9b-68aed4d3b740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306221} {:answer/id #uuid "fb766637-1864-5ffe-9c7e-2a25126f2065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306222} {:answer/id #uuid "40b54c04-fd69-5a26-ab3f-9500095b483d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306223} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c3bb3a-be59-5524-b6e9-d5f530c3b052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306224} {:answer/id #uuid "7917d3fe-72ff-5aaf-8d0a-8364bfed0a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb766637-1864-5ffe-9c7e-2a25126f2065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306222} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a191dc-0130-5fb7-9b64-41f161a6b503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ec40444-64f8-58a7-b09c-d9c17582ccae" :question/math-academy-id "q-140055" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cab45579-9d99-5602-a5de-7ab45da25216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306228} {:answer/id #uuid "16924c86-72f6-53f8-8e7c-719d02b7ca89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306229} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d92175-fa2e-5cf3-83f5-e82cf225cc2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306230} {:answer/id #uuid "e781750e-f339-5376-aa76-feca24822b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306231} {:answer/id #uuid "0f306b0d-86a2-5b31-b6fc-98874112414e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e781750e-f339-5376-aa76-feca24822b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306231} :answer-field/id #uuid "20837c8e-968e-53ae-9a41-e4fb54728aea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "452b1c45-f344-59d0-8f2d-8526fb787055" :question/math-academy-id "q-36914" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. On the other hand, from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53fd241e-b38c-55d8-9f12-a12d08a0e16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338919} {:answer/id #uuid "353e424e-0ac2-568e-ae87-ff7bcb33a26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338920} {:answer/id #uuid "4de4da56-2e52-5786-8a37-4434e69b56db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338921} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d0f0fa-3911-5761-bd3c-7b85a16c8165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338922} {:answer/id #uuid "a792d964-068d-5f40-a5fd-e768ca2cf81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a792d964-068d-5f40-a5fd-e768ca2cf81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923} :answer-field/id #uuid "c82c89df-a0fb-5a2f-8956-b3be5f6ac309" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c022e488-b03e-52fb-b544-84b03490d40c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36929" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-4I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ee1c191-9965-57fa-b29c-defa3d0754d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360854} {:answer/id #uuid "013ffa88-ff96-50ad-ad81-99ede22ff982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360855} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1bf944-0611-5f56-8eee-824bd856ab81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360856} {:answer/id #uuid "47808e53-8f52-5805-acb4-05bc1bab9458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360857} {:answer/id #uuid "af4703df-10f5-532a-a6da-a9d839ab31f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d1bf944-0611-5f56-8eee-824bd856ab81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360856} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d7376df-56b8-587d-bf29-1b3845502a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "474a90c6-ac56-574a-b515-a9ddafabb1ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-37599" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -4 \\\\ 24 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}10-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 24 & -10-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )(-10-\\lambda )-24\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88847457-f4b4-5e2d-9345-dd0a9b98e9a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306281} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc469a9-7937-55be-9104-59d3272e1da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=15,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306282} {:answer/id #uuid "31911b15-83cf-54d3-ad0b-446b9b604eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306283} {:answer/id #uuid "ab82adc1-ef0d-5502-8c8c-cfd158599c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306284} {:answer/id #uuid "28c19d3c-2295-53e4-964b-399ff2f13b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31911b15-83cf-54d3-ad0b-446b9b604eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306283} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3d7af8e-578e-5a22-8502-5334e5c627b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52d32399-93d9-5ce5-a996-5d60c1e0900e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37610" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=15.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e4cc75a-c249-5c14-b6c2-046927dabc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306288} {:ans